      <pub-date pub-type="epub"><year>2022</year></pub-date>
      <abstract><p>We examined the effect of cognitive training on working memory (N = 120).
        Participants were randomly assigned to treatment (n = 60) or control (n = 60).
        We report Cohen's d = 0.45 with 95% CI [0.08, 0.81].</p></abstract>
    </article-meta>
  </front>
  <body>
    <sec>
      <title>Methods</title>
      <p>Participants (N = 120) were randomly assigned to treatment (n = 60) or control (n = 60) conditions.
         A priori power analysis (G*Power) indicated this sample size would provide 80% power to detect a
         medium effect size (d = 0.5) at alpha = .05, two-tailed. Normality was assessed using the
         Shapiro-Wilk test. Levene's test indicated homogeneity of variance, F(1, 118) = 0.34, p = .562.
         Outliers (&gt; 3 SD) were removed. Bonferroni correction was applied for multiple comparisons.</p>
      <p>Analyses were conducted using R version 4.2.1 with the lme4 package. Data are available at OSF
         (https://osf.io/fixture). Analysis code is available at GitHub. Study was pre-registered at OSF.
         Random seed was set to 42.</p>
    </sec>
    <sec>
      <title>Results</title>
      <p>An independent samples t-test revealed a significant difference between groups,
         t(118) = 2.45, p = .016, Cohen's d = 0.45, 95% CI [0.08, 0.81], representing a medium effect size.
         Pearson correlation between training hours and improvement was r = .42, p = .003.
         A one-way ANOVA yielded F(2, 117) = 4.56, p = .012, eta-squared = 0.07.</p>
      <table-wrap id="T1">
        <label>Table 1</label>
        <caption><p>Descriptive statistics by group.</p></caption>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Group</th><th>M</th><th>SD</th><th>n</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Treatment</td><td>M = 4.56</td><td>SD = 1.23</td><td>n = 60</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Control</td><td>M = 3.12</td><td>SD = 1.45</td><td>n = 60</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </table-wrap>
    </sec>
    <sec>
      <title>Discussion</title>
      <p>The findings suggest that cognitive training produces a medium effect on working memory capacity.</p>
    </sec>
